Q: Calendar: adding a day time the button is clicked, in Java Swing A: <h2>Avoid legacy date-time classes</h2>
<p>Avoid the terribly flawed legacy date-time classes. Never use <code>Date</code>, <code>Calendar</code>, <code>GregorianCalendar</code>, <code>SimpleDateFormat</code>, etc.</p>
<p>Use only the modern <em>java.time</em> classes built into Java 8+.</p>
<h2>Wrong type</h2>
<p>Besides being flawed and legacy, <code>Calendar</code> is also the wrong type for your needs. That class represents a moment, a point on the timeline.</p>
<p>If instead you want only the date, use <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> class.</p>
<h2><code>LocalDate</code></h2>
<p>To represent a date-only value, without time-of-day, and without time zone or offset, use <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> class.</p>
<p>To increment the date, just call <code>LocalDate#plusDays</code> adding one day at a time.</p>
<pre><code>this.date = this.date.plusDays( 1 ); // Increment the date.
</code></pre>
<p>Here is an example <code>JFrame</code>.</p>
<p>Change the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/format/FormatStyle.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>FormatStyle</code></a> of the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/format/DateTimeFormatter.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DateTimeFormatter</code></a> to suit your needs, for shorter or longer text.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package work.basil.example;

import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.ActionEvent;
import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.*;
import java.util.Locale;

public class IncrementDaysFrame extends JFrame
{
    // Fields.
    private Locale locale;
    private ZoneId zoneId;
    private DateTimeFormatter formatter;
    private LocalDate date;

    // Widgets.
    final JLabel dateLabel;
    final JButton incrementDateButton;

    // Constructor
    public IncrementDaysFrame ( )
    {
        // --------|  Fields  |-----------------------------
        this.locale = Locale.getDefault();
        this.formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedDate( FormatStyle.FULL ).withLocale( this.locale );
        this.zoneId = ZoneId.systemDefault();
        this.date = LocalDate.now( this.zoneId );

        // --------|  Widgets  |-----------------------------
        this.dateLabel = new JLabel();
        this.dateLabel.setText( &quot;Today: &quot; + this.formatter.format( this.date ) ); // Initial value.
        this.incrementDateButton = new JButton( &quot;Increment&quot; );
        this.incrementDateButton.addActionListener( ( ActionEvent actionEvent ) -&gt; {
            this.date = this.date.plusDays( 1 ); // Increment the date.
            this.dateLabel.setText( this.formatter.format( this.date ) );
        } );

        // --------|  Arrange  |-----------------------------

        // Basic window setup
        this.setTitle( &quot;Increment Date Example&quot; );
        this.setSize( 300 , 160 );
        this.setLocationRelativeTo( null ); // center on screen

        // Use modern look &amp; feel (optional but nicer)
        try { UIManager.setLookAndFeel( UIManager.getSystemLookAndFeelClassName() ); } catch ( Exception e ) { System.err.println( &quot;Could not set look and feel&quot; ); }

        // Main content panel with some padding
        JPanel mainPanel = new JPanel();
        mainPanel.setBorder( BorderFactory.createEmptyBorder( 30 , 30 , 30 , 30 ) );
        mainPanel.setLayout( new BoxLayout( mainPanel , BoxLayout.Y_AXIS ) );
        mainPanel.add( this.dateLabel );
        mainPanel.add( this.incrementDateButton );


        // FYI: The default layout manager of `JFrame` is `BorderLayout`.
        this.add( mainPanel , BorderLayout.CENTER );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And here is an app to exercise that frame code.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package work.basil.example;

import javax.swing.*;

public class AppIncrementingDates
{
    // Main method.
    static void main ( )
    {
        // --------|  Business Logic  |---------------------
        final AppIncrementingDates app = new AppIncrementingDates();

        // --------|  GUI  |---------------------
        // Run GUI on the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT). Crucial!
        // Access the GUI only from that EDT, never from any other thread.
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater( ( ) -&gt;
        {
            IncrementDaysFrame mainFrame = app.createAndShowGui();
        } );
    }

    private IncrementDaysFrame createAndShowGui ( )
    {
        IncrementDaysFrame mainFrame = new IncrementDaysFrame();

        // The user clicks the Close button on the window.
        mainFrame.setDefaultCloseOperation( JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE );

        mainFrame.setVisible( true );
        return mainFrame;
    }
}
</code></pre>
